KG42.register("rules-362e4b7bfe22327ae880d993",{"id":"38-bidirectional_ev_trading::v141-c-R002","paper":"38-bidirectional_ev_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R002","mechanism_id":"M01","name":"Local market coordinator","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"The aggregator may update and broadcast other customers' sum energy consumption through communication with the customer's ECS. Scope: Collaborative distributed scheduling using the sequential customer updates of problem P1_i. Condition: At the beginning of each iteration.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.local_operator under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.local_operator"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Collaborative distributed scheduling using the sequential customer updates of problem P1_i.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-Xneg-i","symbol":"X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}","meaning":"Sum energy consumption of all customers other than customer i at time-slot t in the distributed collaborative procedure: the quantity the aggregator updates and broadcasts to customer i's ECS at the beginning of iteration k+1. It is a derived aggregate (defined in C002), not an independent decision of any agent.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued for each t in T (kWh), fixed by the summands' values through the defining formula in C002; the source states no separate bound or sign restriction on X_{-i} itself in this passage","unit":"kWh","indices":["i in I","t in T","k (iteration index)"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-e03","v141-b-g02-e04"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-i-tot","symbol":"x_{i,Tot}^{t}","meaning":"Customer i's scheduled total energy consumption during time-slot t, including the energy consumed by residential appliances and the EV; it is negative when the corresponding amount of energy is sold to the aggregator. In this rule it appears only as the summand of the broadcast aggregate X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)} (with iteration superscripts (k) or (k+1) as printed in the source formula), i.e. as the data being aggregated, not as a decision taken by the local coordinator.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"customer i's feasible scheduling set X_i; in particular x_{i,Tot}^{min,t} \u003c= x_{i,Tot}^{t} \u003c= x_{i,Tot}^{max,t} for all t in T (equation (2)), with x_{i,Tot}^{min,t} = 0 for Type I/II EVs and x_{i,Tot}^{min,t} \u003c 0 permitted for a Type III EV; real-valued, may be negative when energy is sold to the aggregator","unit":"kWh","indices":["i in I","t in T","k / k+1 (iteration index, as printed)"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E04"]}],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The aggregator may update and broadcast other customers' sum energy consumption through communication with the customer's ECS. Scope: Collaborative distributed scheduling using the sequential customer updates of problem P1_i. Condition: At the beginning of each iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-Xneg-i"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g01-E12"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"In the per-customer subproblem P1_i that this broadcast procedure serves, the sum energy consumption of all customers other than customer i is defined for every time-slot t in T as the total energy consumption of the customers already updated at iteration k+1 (indices i' \u003c i) plus the total energy consumption of the customers not yet updated, taken at iteration k (indices i' > i). This is the quantity the aggregator updates and broadcasts to customer i's ECS at the beginning of each iteration.","expression":"X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)} = \\sum_{i'\u003ci} x_{i',Tot}^{t,(k+1)} + \\sum_{i'>i} x_{i',Tot}^{t,(k)}, \\quad \\forall t \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-Xneg-i","v141-c-R002-x-i-tot"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E12","v141-b-g02-e03","v141-b-g02-e04"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"1222","section":"III. Energy Scheduling: Collaborative Approach","anchor_text":"of where Xt,(k+1)−i = i′\u003ci xt,(k+1)i′,T ot + i′>i xt,(k)i′,T ot. Problem sh P1i is a convex optimiza","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Note that at each iteration, each customer needs to acquire only the information about the wholesale price function p^t(\\cdot), \\forall t\\in T and the sum energy consumption of other customers, X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}, \\forall t\\in T. Such information can be updated and broadcast by the aggregator through the communication with the customer's ECS at the beging of each iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":", pp tial appliances, xti,App. Compared to residential appliances that only consume energy, the EV h","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"focusing on the energy trading among customers, we consider a simplified appliance model where the amount of energy consumed by all the residential appliances of customer i during time-slot t is denoted by a single variable x_{i,App}^t. Then, we model customer i's utility function for its residential appliances during time-slot t as an increasing concave function U_i^t(x_{i,App}^t) of the amount of energy consumed by the residential appliances, x_{i,App}^t. Compared to residential appliances that only consume energy, the EV has the capability to charge and discharge energy into/from its built-in battery. The discharged energy can be used by other appliances, and thus x_{i,App}^t includes the energy consumed from the battery in the EV as well as the aggregator. We denote the total amount of customer i's energy consumption during time-slot t by x_{i,Tot}^t with the inclusion of the energy consumed by both the residential appliances and the EV. Accordingly, the difference between x_{i,Tot}^t and x_{i,App}^t, i.e., x_{i,Tot}^t - x_{i,App}^t, is the amount of energy charged/discharged into/from the EV's battery during time-slot t. Positive difference, x_{i,Tot}^t - x_{i,App}^t > 0, represents that the corresponding amount of energy is charged to the EV's battery during time-slot t, while negative difference, x_{i,Tot}^t - x_{i,App}^t \u003c 0, represents that the corresponding amount of energy is discharged from the EV's battery. We denote the overall energy scheduling of customer i by \\bar{x} = [\\bar{x}_i]_{i\\in I}, where \\bar{x}_i = [x_{i,Tot}^t, x_{i,App}^t]_{t\\in T}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"overall energy scheduling of customer i by x = [xi]i∈I, where tri ¯xi = [xti,T ot, xti,App]t∈T . ch ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We allow the bidirectional energy trading between customers and the aggregator, i.e., each customer can sell its energy to the aggregator when it has surplus energy stored in its EV's battery. Due to the energy trading capability, x_{i,Tot}^t may have a negative value, which means that the corresponding amount of energy is sold to the aggregator. For example, if x_{i,App}^t = 5 kWh and x_{i,Tot}^t = -10 kWh (accordingly x_{i,Tot}^t - x_{i,App}^t = -15 kWh), customer i discharges 15 kWh of energy from its battery to use 5 kWh for its own appliances and to sell 10 kWh to the aggregator at t-th time-slot."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p03-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"SCHEDULING WITH ELECTRIC VEHICLES IN THE SMART GRID 1221 both maximum and minimum amounts of energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"both maximum and minimum amounts of energy which the residential appliances and the customer i's household can consume during each time-slot, i.e., x_{i,App}^{min,t} \\le x_{i,App}^t \\le x_{i,App}^{max,t}, \\forall t\\in T (1) x_{i,Tot}^{min,t} \\le x_{i,Tot}^t \\le x_{i,Tot}^{max,t}, \\forall t\\in T. (2) The amount of charged (or discharged) energy of the EV during each time-slot is also constrained by the maximum and minimum values, i.e., x_{i,EV}^{min,t} \\le x_{i,Tot}^t - x_{i,App}^t \\le x_{i,EV}^{max,t}, \\forall t\\in T. (3)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Energy Scheduling: Collaborative Approach","anchor_text":"of where Xt,(k+1)−i = i′\u003ci xt,(k+1)i′,T ot + i′>i xt,(k)i′,T ot. Problem sh P1i is a convex optimiza","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"At each iteration k+1, customers update there energy scheduling decisions, \\bar{x}_i^{(k+1)}, sequentially by solving the following problem: P1_i \\max_{\\bar{x}_i}\\sum_{t\\in T}U_i^t(x_{i,App}^t)-\\sum_{t\\in T}C^t(x_{i,Tot}^t+X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}) s.t. \\bar{x}_i\\in X_i, where X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}=\\sum_{i'\u003ci}x_{i',Tot}^{t,(k+1)}+\\sum_{i'>i}x_{i',Tot}^{t,(k)}. Problem P1_i is a convex optimization problem and can be solved by using standard algorithms for convex optimization."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"1222","section":"III. Energy Scheduling: Collaborative Approach","anchor_text":"of any customer i, when the other customers scheduling ag decisions, ¯x−i = [¯xi′]i′̸=i, are ﬁxed. D","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\bar{x}_i^{(k+1)} = \\arg\\max_{\\bar{x}_i\\in X_i} \\phi(\\bar{x}_1^{(k+1)}, \\ldots, \\bar{x}_{i-1}^{(k+1)}, \\bar{x}_i, \\bar{x}_{i+1}^{(k)}, \\ldots, \\bar{x}_{|I|}^{(k)}), \\quad \\forall i\\in I, \\quad (8) where |Z| denotes the cardinality of set Z. At each iteration k+1, customers update there energy scheduling decisions, \\bar{x}_i^{(k+1)}, sequencially by solving the following problem: P1_i \\max_{\\bar{x}_i} \\sum_{t\\in T} U_i^t(x_{i,App}^t) - \\sum_{t\\in T} C^t(x_{i,Tot}^t + X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}) \\quad \\text{s.t. } \\bar{x}_i\\in X_i, where X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)} = \\sum_{i'\u003ci} x_{i',Tot}^{t,(k+1)} + \\sum_{i'>i} x_{i',Tot}^{t,(k)}. Problem P1_i is a convex optimization problem and can be solved by using standard algorithms for convex optimization."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":"No parameter is declared because the R002 source sentence names only two broadcast items: the wholesale price function p^t(.) and the sum of other customers' energy consumption X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}. The only constant-like object, the wholesale price function with predetermined coefficients a^t > 0 and b^t > 0, is scoped to the price-signal rule v141-c-R003 and is not part of the frozen R002 statement, nodes or edges; declaring it here would assert a price-signal relation this rule does not own. The remaining symbols in the rule are indices/sets (i, i', t, k, I, T), which are not parameters in this schema."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","quote":"the sum energy consumption of other customers, X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}, \\forall t\\in T. Such information can be updated and broadcast by the aggregator through the communication with the customer's ECS at the beging of each iteration."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"f33f4db255c9092d7c1bbfeced3c50e00c4774e8a69d34275c561d8497ed1651","applicability":"formalized","reason":"v141-c-R002 is a coordination-stage information-exchange rule whose frozen graph contains the single node coordination.local_operator and no edges, i.e. a unary fact. C001 ('the aggregator may update and broadcast other customers' sum energy consumption through communication with the customer's ECS, at the beginning of each iteration') is stated in prose and is kept as an unformalized procedural duty. The one mathematical object inside this rule's scope is the broadcast aggregate X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}; the source defines it explicitly as the sum of the already-updated customers' total consumption at iteration k+1 plus the not-yet-updated customers' consumption at iteration k, and that definition is transcribed exactly as supporting constraint C002. Two declared objects are required to write it: the derived broadcast aggregate and the customer total energy consumption appearing as its summand; the latter is declared here only with the explicit Section III / problem P1_i iteration scope and is not claimed to be owned by this rule. No theta are declared: the wholesale price function p^t(.) that appears in the same evidence sentence is scoped to the price-signal rule v141-c-R003, and the frozen R002 graph contains no price-signal node or relation, so re-declaring it here would import a relation this rule does not own. All B evidence used below remains unreviewed; no independent source audit or downstream scientific acceptance is claimed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-x-Xneg-i","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The broadcast aggregate is exactly the information the local coordinator updates and pushes to each customer's ECS, so it attaches to the only node in this rule's frozen graph. The frozen graph declares no edge for R002, consistent with a unary information-duty fact; no relation is invented."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-x-i-tot","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"x_{i,Tot}^{t} enters this rule only as a summand inside the aggregate that coordination.local_operator broadcasts; the customer's own scheduling decision belongs to the clearing rule (v141-c-R001). It is declared here with the explicit Section III / problem P1_i iteration scope stated in the evidence, not by cross-rule reuse."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The procedural update-and-broadcast duty is a property of the local coordinator node; the frozen R002 edge set is empty, so the constraint is bound to the node only."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The definition fixes the content of the aggregate that the local coordinator broadcasts at each iteration; it is a supporting condition on the same single node and requires no edge."}],"revision_requests":["No blocking revision is required: the frozen unary fact (coordination.local_operator, no edges) is sufficient to carry the procedural broadcast duty and the definition of the broadcast aggregate.","For transparency: the underlying evidence sentence bundles the wholesale price function p^t(.) with X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)} as information the aggregator may broadcast, but R002 has no edge to coordination.price_signal; the price broadcast is instead represented in v141-c-R003 (edge v141-c-R003-E001). If the broadcast relation is intended to belong to R002 as well, C.1 would need to add that edge with same-mechanism evidence (v141-b-g02-e04 and v141-b-g01-E14) before it can be formalized here.","All B evidence cited above carries source_status unverified under the experimental unreviewed-candidate policy; the algebra of C002 is a faithful transcription of the supplied quotation but has not passed independent source audit."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
